 Plan: env.clipboardHistory Extension API
  
 Context

 VS Code's env.clipboard API exposes only readText() / writeText(). Extensions that implement paste-history features, smart completions, or clipboard inspectors have no way to query what has been written to the clipboard
 during a session. This plan adds env.getClipboardHistory() — a pull-based async API that returns an ordered list of clipboard entries written through VS Code during the current session.

 Scope note: History is tracked at the IClipboardService level inside VS Code, so it captures all clipboard writes made through VS Code (editor copy, terminal copy, extension writeText calls). It does not track native OS
 clipboard changes made by other apps — that would require OS polling and is out of scope.

 Target repo: github.com/microsoft/vscode (the local repo at vscode_sg only contains extensions/ — a full clone of microsoft/vscode is required to implement this).

 ---
 New Public Surface (src/vscode-dts/vscode.d.ts)

 Add adjacent to the existing Clipboard interface:

 /**
  * Represents a single clipboard history entry.
  */
 export interface ClipboardHistoryItem {
     /** The text value that was written to the clipboard. */
     readonly value: string;
     /** Unix timestamp (ms) of when the entry was written. */
     readonly timestamp: number;
 }

 export namespace env {
     // existing: clipboard: Clipboard;

     /**
      * Returns the clipboard write history for this VS Code session,
      * most recent entry first. Maximum 50 entries are retained.
      */
     export function getClipboardHistory(): Thenable<readonly ClipboardHistoryItem[]>;
 }

 ---
 Step-by-Step Implementation

 Step 1 — Extend IClipboardService with history tracking

 File: src/vs/platform/clipboard/common/clipboardService.ts

 Add an optional onDidWriteText event to the interface (keeps implementations backward-compatible):

 // in IClipboardService interface
 readonly onDidWriteText?: Event<string>;

 Then in each platform implementation that calls writeText:
 - src/vs/platform/clipboard/browser/clipboardService.ts — BrowserClipboardService: fire the event after a successful write.
 - src/vs/workbench/services/clipboard/electron-browser/clipboardService.ts — NativeClipboardService: fire after nativeHostService.writeClipboardText() resolves.

 ---
 Step 2 — Protocol shapes (extHost.protocol.ts)

 File: src/vs/workbench/api/common/extHost.protocol.ts

 Add $readHistory() to the existing shape, and add ClipboardHistoryItem as a transferred DTO:

 // extend existing shape
 export interface MainThreadClipboardShape extends IDisposable {
     $readText(): Promise<string>;
     $writeText(value: string): Promise<void>;
     $readHistory(): Promise<{ value: string; timestamp: number }[]>; // ← NEW
 }

 No new channel identifier needed — this goes on the existing MainContext.MainThreadClipboard proxy.

 ---
 Step 3 — Main-process implementation (mainThreadClipboard.ts)

 File: src/vs/workbench/api/browser/mainThreadClipboard.ts

 Keep a ring-buffer of history entries. Hook IClipboardService.onDidWriteText at construction time:

 private readonly _history: { value: string; timestamp: number }[] = [];
 private static readonly MAX_HISTORY = 50;

 constructor(
     _context: IExtHostContext,
     @IClipboardService private readonly _clipboardService: IClipboardService,
 ) {
     // existing...
     this._clipboardService.onDidWriteText?.(text => {
         this._history.unshift({ value: text, timestamp: Date.now() });
         if (this._history.length > MainThreadClipboard.MAX_HISTORY) {
             this._history.pop();
         }
     });
 }

 $readHistory(): Promise<{ value: string; timestamp: number }[]> {
     return Promise.resolve([...this._history]);
 }

 ---
 Step 4 — Extension-host side (extHostClipboard.ts)

 File: src/vs/workbench/api/common/extHostClipboard.ts

 Add getClipboardHistory() method alongside the existing value (the Clipboard object):

 export class ExtHostClipboard {
     readonly value: vscode.Clipboard;
     private readonly _proxy: MainThreadClipboardShape;

     constructor(mainContext: IMainContext) {
         this._proxy = mainContext.getProxy(MainContext.MainThreadClipboard);
         this.value = Object.freeze({
             readText: () => this._proxy.$readText(),
             writeText: (v: string) => this._proxy.$writeText(v),
         });
     }

     getHistory(): Promise<readonly vscode.ClipboardHistoryItem[]> {
         return this._proxy.$readHistory();
     }
 }

 ---
 Step 5 — Wire into vscode namespace (extHost.api.impl.ts)

 File: src/vs/workbench/api/common/extHost.api.impl.ts

 In createApiFactoryAndRegisterActors, find the env namespace builder (around line 418 where clipboard is wired) and add:

 // existing
 get clipboard(): vscode.Clipboard { return extHostClipboard.value; },

 // NEW
 getClipboardHistory(): Thenable<readonly vscode.ClipboardHistoryItem[]> {
     return extHostClipboard.getHistory();
 },

